le for refilling reserves. Interconnector setting opens remote delivery capacity, whose temperature depends on recent flows. Renewables can be curtailed at that connection. Conventional governors respond to frequency with finite response times and output limits.\nA reset samples only the initial physical observable values from these ranges: {\"frequency\": [49.8, 50.2], \"load\": [90.0, 120.0], \"renewable_share\": [0.2, 0.4]}. Initial reported measurements include the same observation-noise process used during research. Unobserved memory always starts from the same fixed reference rule described in the brief, including any deterministic dependence on the initial observable state. There is no random prehistory. Public and final hidden episodes use this same reset rule."}, {"id": "power_grid-research-and-forecasting", "title": "What remains fixed", "text": "Every team investigates the same underlying physical parameters and active mechanisms for this system. Those exact parameters and mechanisms are used for public and final evaluation. Each phase contains 40 secret episodes of 4000 steps: ten sustained-operation, ten intervention-order, ten recovery-spacing and ten joint-intervention episodes. All actions stay within the public bounds. Only the histories and starting observations are hidden before upload. The predictor receives the complete future physical action schedule."}], "forecast_context": {"protocol": "forecaster-v1", "revision": "research-forecast-v8", "family": "power_grid", "observables": ["load", "frequency", "renewable_share"], "intervention_bounds": {"price_signal": [0.0, 2.0], "reserve_dispatch": [0.0, 150.0], "charging_allowance": [0.0, 1.0], "interconnector": [0.0, 1.0]}, "brief": "Grid load in power units, frequency in Hz, and renewable share of delivered generation. Price signal reduces desired demand; reserve dispatch requests additional supply in power units. A tick is one dispatch interval. Supply requests can be limited by available physical r